    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. so if the ldl is high and it's building up in my arteries, what is actually happening in there? like how does it physically affect the heart over time?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When high LDL builds up, it infiltrates the artery walls to be temporarily stored, triggering an inflammatory response from your immune system that summons macrophages. These cells absorb the LDL but cannot digest it, turning into fatty \"foam cells\" that coalesce into soft plaques, narrowing the vessel and hardening the artery wall into atherosclerosis. Over time, this buildup restricts blood flow to the heart muscle or can rupture suddenly, causing clots that lead to heart attacks or strokes.",
      "source": "target",
